+### The organisers' reply on prior work, 30 Oct 2026 (branch `feat/demo-polish`)
+
+The owner asked the hackathon's organisers, in Discord, whether a project that began before the event as a college team project, and has been extended alone since 26 Oct, is eligible if the demo shows only what was built during the event, and whether the video may show an older screen, labelled, as the way into a new feature. At the owner's instruction the question named no repository and gave no link: the project was not to be revealed before it was submitted. The reply came the same day from the Cerebral Valley app account, so it is an automated answer and not a named person's, and it restates the rules summary the owner had pasted earlier. It is transcribed in `docs/HACKATHON.md` §9.1.
+
+- **What it says.** (1) The entry can be eligible, but the submitted repository must be a fresh public one, created on 26 Oct or later, holding only work done during the event, with the earlier code and its git history left out. That code may stay in a separate repository, private or public, if the README names it and explains how it is used. (2) A brief labelled shot of an older screen is fine if the video opens with one line saying what existed before and what was built this week; the demo itself shows only event work.
+- **What changed in the docs.** `docs/HACKATHON.md`: the open question became "The organisers' answer" (§9.1) with the reply transcribed; its checklist box is ticked, with the caveat that it came from an app account; a new unchecked box covers the submission repository, and a new §9.3 describes it; §10 opens with the one line the organisers asked for (shot 1 replaced the village hook, and shot 2 now says "The borrower taps the microphone and says it in Hindi."). The clause "says once that the rules engine under it is prior work from September" left the §10 intro and the video box, because the opening line covers it; the one sentence about the old result page in shot 5 stays. The shot words went from 319 to 322, about 2:09 at 150 words a minute and about 159 s of speech in 170 s, counted the same way as before. `CLAUDE.md` gained the pointer to §9.3 and a sentence on its Repo line: this repository becomes the named earlier code and has to stay public with its history intact.
+- **The arrangement.** A separate repository (first local only, named `ArthNiti-event`; created on GitHub the same day, see the next entry) holds `added/` (the 51 files the event created), `patches/` (83 zero-context patches, one for each earlier file the event changed), `pack.json` (also the 3 deleted files and the git tree hash of the finished app), `assemble.mjs`, a README, the MIT licence, CI and the two maintainer scripts. `node assemble.mjs` downloads one pinned commit of this repository, `e4708306cab1`, applies the patches, and exits 0 only if the result is byte for byte the author's tree. Its history replays the event commits with their dates unchanged. Every state was rebuilt and matched, and a fresh clone made under Windows line-ending conversion rebuilt too. Creating the GitHub repository, naming it and making it public are the owner's calls.
+- **Not settled.** Whether a repository that rebuilds the app from a pinned commit of this one counts as one that "holds only work done during the event"; whether the hours of 26 Oct before the 12:00 PM ET start count as event time (answered later the same day, next entry: the line is 12:24 PM ET); whether an automated reply matches the Official Rules. The owner can put the first two to a human organiser. Some of the event commits have never been pushed here, so publishing the new repository publishes their content for the first time. This repository has to stay public with `e4708306cab1` reachable (no force-push, no history rewrite, no rename), because the rebuild downloads it and the earlier team members may still hold clones.
